WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.640
Whether they feature shocking twists or important&nbsp;
character developments, these sequel and prequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.534
moments made it impossible for us to watch our&nbsp;
favorite movies and shows the same way again.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.480
It's safe to say that the truth about Darth&nbsp;
Vader — that he used to be Anakin Skywalker — is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:20.700
no longer much of a spoiler, but it sure was&nbsp;
back in 1980. It's probably the most famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.800
twist in sci-fi history — and it wasn't even&nbsp;
in the filmmakers' original plans. Judging by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.600
earlier versions of the script, George Lucas had&nbsp;
initially intended for Darth Vader and Anakin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.460
be two separate characters. The idea didn't even&nbsp;
occur to him until he was working on "The Empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.500
Strikes Back" — and the twist was originally&nbsp;
proposed as a joke, according to Marcia Lucas.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.540
Yet amazingly, so many things from the original&nbsp;
movie make sense in retrospect. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.920
fans can rewatch the scene from "A New&nbsp;
Hope" where Obi-Wan tells Luke about Anakin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.720
and find several clues that suggest the old master&nbsp;
wasn't telling the whole truth — even if they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.700
purely coincidental. When Obi-Wan tells Luke&nbsp;
about Uncle Owen's relationship with Anakin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.234
his delivery suggests that he might&nbsp;
be hiding something important.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.280
"He didn't hold with your father's ideals, thought&nbsp;
he should've stayed here and not gotten involved."

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.400
Likewise, when Luke asks how his father died,&nbsp;
Obi-Wan pauses noticeably before answering. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.780
is technically lying when he tells Luke that&nbsp;
Vader "betrayed and murdered" Anakin — but if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.100
you want to be more generous, you could say&nbsp;
that Obi-Wan is simply being metaphorical.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:29.040
Since the beginning of the MCU, one thing has&nbsp;
seemed certain: SHIELD is on the side of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.240
good guys. After all, the organization&nbsp;
assembled the Avengers and consistently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.260
worked against malevolent villains like&nbsp;
Loki and the evil HYDRA organization.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.660
However, "Captain America: The Winter Soldier"&nbsp;
changed all that, as Steve Rogers discovers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:47.220
that SHIELD and HYDRA have become one and the&nbsp;
same. Since the events of the first "Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.780
America" movie, HYDRA has slowly infiltrated&nbsp;
the ranks of SHIELD — and they're hijacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:57.180
SHIELD's weapons and resources to carry out its&nbsp;
agenda. The sequel forces Captain America to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:02.040
begin questioning authority and reexamine his&nbsp;
assumptions about right and wrong. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.140
it made Marvel fans feel a little guilty for&nbsp;
cheering at SHIELD's previous victories. Fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.460
will realize with horror that HYDRA must have&nbsp;
been working behind the scenes the whole time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.980
even during the Battle of&nbsp;
New York in "The Avengers."

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.560
"HYDRA grew right under your&nbsp;
nose, and nobody noticed."&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.440
"Why do you think we're meeting&nbsp;
in this cave? I noticed."&nbsp;

00:02:20.081 --> 00:02:21.821
"How many paid the price before you did?"

00:02:21.821 --> 00:02:26.820
It's also worth noting that "Captain America: The&nbsp;
Winter Soldier" set an entirely new precedent for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:31.440
the MCU. Fans no longer had to wait for the&nbsp;
next Avengers movie to see plot points that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.900
would have a major impact on other Marvel films.&nbsp;
This sequel proved that the events of one solo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.856
movie could ripple outward to affect the entire&nbsp;
MCU — something we've seen many times since.

00:02:42.856 --> 00:02:47.880
In "Finding Nemo," Dory's short-term memory&nbsp;
loss is pretty much just a running gag. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.700
it moves the plot forward and helps&nbsp;
Marlin become a better parent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.020
but it's still mostly played for laughs.&nbsp;
Upon watching "Finding Dory," though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.280
fans will discover that the lovable blue&nbsp;
tang has her own tragic backstory to tell.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.960
"Finding Dory" shows how much of a nightmare&nbsp;
it can be to live in constant fear that you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.220
forget about the people you love. Viewers&nbsp;
can feel the pain of Dory's parents as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:12.240
worry about how their daughter will be able to&nbsp;
take care of herself — and for the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.800
audiences know what it's like to see the&nbsp;
world through Dory's eyes. In particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.580
the montage at the beginning of the movie,&nbsp;
which shows young Dory growing up alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:24.540
cements to viewers how little they truly&nbsp;
understood Dory in the first movie.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:28.740
These moments from "Finding Dory" add a&nbsp;
whole new layer to "Finding Nemo" if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.820
watch it again. Not only do we learn the&nbsp;
origins of her "Just Keep Swimming" song,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:37.380
but her monologue in which she begs Marlin not&nbsp;
to leave her becomes even more poignant when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.280
you realize just how many people&nbsp;
have left Dory before. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.760
some of the jokes from the first film&nbsp;
will take on a much sadder undertone.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.180
"I forget things almost instantly.&nbsp;
It runs in my family. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.596 --> 00:03:52.207
at least I think it does. Uh… hm. Where are they?"

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.960
Fans who loved the faithful android&nbsp;
Bishop in "Aliens" are undoubtedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:02.760
devastated when the character dies in "Alien&nbsp;
3." However, in the climax of the third movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:08.160
they find themselves unsettled rather than joyous&nbsp;
when Bishop appears to be back from the dead. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:13.320
turns out that this isn't Bishop — rather, it's&nbsp;
the man who built him. Known only as "Bishop II,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:18.300
this employee of the Weyland-Yutani Corporation&nbsp;
designed the android to look exactly like him.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:23.340
While the two characters share the same face, they&nbsp;
couldn't be more different. Bishop is willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:28.200
sacrifice himself to save Ripley, while Bishop II&nbsp;
cares only about harvesting the xenomorph inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.940
of her. Bishop II isn't an android programmed&nbsp;
to carry out the bidding of the company;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:37.800
he's an actual human being who sees the&nbsp;
xenomorph as a "magnificent specimen."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:41.400
Bishop II is just as cunning and&nbsp;
callous as Ash from the first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:46.680
but what makes this man somehow worse is that&nbsp;
you almost want to believe him. How can you not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.880
when he has Bishop's face? If you rewatch&nbsp;
"Aliens" after seeing this moment from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.670
"Alien 3," you'll never be able to&nbsp;
look at Bishop the same way again.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:59.340
Norman from "Don't Breathe" certainly seems&nbsp;
like a victim at first glance. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.780
he's just a blind man who comes across as pretty&nbsp;
defenseless after three teenagers break into his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:09.360
home. Yet, as the movie unfolds, it becomes&nbsp;
increasingly clear that Norman is the villain.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:14.340
The sequel, "Don't Breathe 2," subverts&nbsp;
expectations once again. Early in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:19.200
viewers see the young girl Phoenix running from&nbsp;
Norman's dog. It seems like Phoenix will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:24.000
Norman's next victim… until the movie reveals that&nbsp;
Phoenix is actually Norman's adoptive daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.340
and he is simply training her how to survive.&nbsp;
Fans may be surprised to discover that Norman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.900
is actually a protective, albeit icy&nbsp;
dad. It's especially shocking to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:37.260
Phoenix be friendly to the dog that&nbsp;
was so terrifying in the original film.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.240
"Snap my fingers, and he'll&nbsp;
bite your testicles off."

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:47.100
Now Norman is shown as less a villain and&nbsp;
more of an antihero. It seems he has moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:51.420
away from kidnapping and rape, and has found a&nbsp;
more tasteful way of having a child of his own:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:55.800
he rescued Phoenix from a fire and&nbsp;
raised her himself. Norman is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.000
definitely ruthless and terrifying, but he's&nbsp;
not quite the monster he was in the prequel.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.980
It may be difficult for viewers to reconcile these&nbsp;
two sides of Norman. Whether you feel that "Don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.360
Breathe 2" is humanizing a three-dimensional&nbsp;
character or trying to excuse the actions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.040
a despicable one, one thing's for sure — it will&nbsp;
completely change how you watch the first film.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:19.980
Jimmy McGill — more commonly known as Saul Goodman&nbsp;
— may just seem like a crooked lawyer in "Breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:25.260
Bad," but the prequel series "Better Call Saul"&nbsp;
adds a whole new layer to his character. Before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:31.140
becoming Albuquerque's scummiest attorney, Jimmy&nbsp;
is deeply insecure: he craves approval from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:35.460
brother Chuck, a once-great lawyer who never&nbsp;
leaves the house anymore due to his "allergy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:39.960
to electricity." Even at his lowest, though,&nbsp;
Chuck is still more respected than Jimmy.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:44.340
But the scene from "Better Call Saul" that&nbsp;
reveals the most about Jimmy is the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.200
from the sixth and final season when Kim breaks&nbsp;
up with him. It's especially heartbreaking because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:53.640
she admits that really she does still love&nbsp;
him — but they can never be together again.

00:06:54.034 --> 00:07:00.214
"But we are bad for everyone around&nbsp;
us. Other people suffer because of us."

00:07:00.214 --> 00:07:04.320
The breakup is the final straw that&nbsp;
completes Jimmy's transformation into Saul.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:09.000
Knowing this about Saul, his callous behavior&nbsp;
in "Breaking Bad" is a bit more understandable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.680
With such a tragic backstory, it's easy&nbsp;
to see why he's willing to sell out his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.580
own clients — like when he urges Walter&nbsp;
to kill Badger in order to save his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:21.420
skin. This may also explain why Saul&nbsp;
is flirting with Skylar one minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:26.100
and then telling her he can't imagine how she&nbsp;
and Walter are still married the next. Perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:30.000
Saul is a bit jealous that Walter managed to&nbsp;
maintain his marriage for as long as he did.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:35.040
Aside from being a mightily satisfying&nbsp;
payoff for Marvel's Infinity Saga, "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.720
Endgame" also enriches the previous films in&nbsp;
the MCU by changing how we view moments from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:44.700
other movies. The time heist at the center of&nbsp;
the film allows fans to revisit several iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:49.500
MCU scenes alongside their favorite heroes — but&nbsp;
this time, they're seen from different angles.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.280
For instance, the introduction of&nbsp;
"Guardians of the Galaxy's" Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:57.780
Quill is hilariously put in perspective when we&nbsp;
see it through the eyes of Nebula and Rhodey.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:01.560
"So he's an idiot?"
"Yeah."

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:06.000
Meanwhile, the trip to 2012 New York has&nbsp;
some more surprises in store. Who would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.900
have guessed there would be an epic Captain&nbsp;
America vs. Captain America battle in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:14.460
Avengers Tower that was conveniently omitted&nbsp;
from "The Avengers?" In addition, the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.900
reminds us that the Hydra-controlled SHIELD had&nbsp;
always been lurking in the background of the MCU.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:24.060
Perhaps the most head-spinning reveal is that,&nbsp;
after the events of "Endgame," Steve Rogers goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:29.160
back in time so he can be reunited with Peggy and&nbsp;
grow old. This means that Peggy Carter's husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.780
as mentioned in "The Winter Soldier," is none&nbsp;
other than Cap. The screenwriters of "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:38.208
Endgame" have confirmed that Rogers&nbsp;
was indeed the father of Peggy's kids.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:43.140
When choosing a villain for the movie "Star Trek&nbsp;
II: The Wrath of Khan," the filmmakers turned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:48.180
the original TV series. After defeating the&nbsp;
genetic superman Khan in the episode "Space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:54.060
Seed," Captain Kirk exiles Khan and his people&nbsp;
to the uninhabited planet Ceti Alpha V. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:57.780
Kirk never bothers to check on them,&nbsp;
so he doesn't realize that Khan ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:02.100
up spending a decade and a half in an&nbsp;
uninhabitable wasteland. The sequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:06.360
film demonstrates that Kirk's well-meaning&nbsp;
actions can have far-reaching consequences.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:11.460
The sequel sets a totally different tone from&nbsp;
the original series right from the opening scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:16.320
in which Lieutenant Saavik makes the decision&nbsp;
to rescue the imperiled Kobayashi Maru from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:21.480
a neutral zone — endangering the entire crew of&nbsp;
the Enterprise. For a moment, it seems like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:26.280
Klingons have killed everybody, including Spock&nbsp;
— but a surprise entrance from Kirk reveals that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:30.780
the whole thing was just a simulation. Although&nbsp;
viewers are relieved to learn that none of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:35.520
they just saw was real, the opening sequence shows&nbsp;
fans a darker and more mature version of the "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.560
Trek" universe. It's a painful reminder that&nbsp;
the crew of the Enterprise might one day need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:45.540
face a "no-win scenario" like the one shown in the&nbsp;
simulation — and that everyone's a redshirt at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:50.700
end of the day. Spock actually dies for real later&nbsp;
in the movie — and even though he's resurrected in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:55.560
the sequels, both his fake and real deaths have&nbsp;
had lasting impacts on the "Star Trek" universe.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:59.220
A running gag in "The Big Bang Theory"&nbsp;
is the "Roommate Agreement" that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:03.780
been established between Sheldon and Leonard.&nbsp;
This is a contract that the two roommates have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:07.020
signed to spell out what they can and&nbsp;
cannot do while living together — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:11.220
Sheldon never hesitates to cite the exact&nbsp;
clauses that he feels Leonard is violating.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:15.120
"I'm here because you violated our&nbsp;
roommate agreement — specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:19.920
Section 8: Visitors, Subsection&nbsp;
C: Females, Paragraph 4: Coitus."

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.720
A scene from "Young Sheldon" explains where&nbsp;
Sheldon first got this idea as a kid — and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:28.920
allows fans to understand why Sheldon cares&nbsp;
so much about contracts into his adulthood.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:32.640
According to the episode "Vanilla&nbsp;
Ice Cream, Gentleman Callers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:37.320
and a Dinette Set," Sheldon's first contract is&nbsp;
created to help his Meemaw while she's dating Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:42.600
Sturgis. Sheldon can't help but overhear the&nbsp;
two arguing — and he also can't help spying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:46.740
on them with binoculars. He wants things to&nbsp;
work out between Meemaw and her new boyfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:51.423
so he offers to draft a relationship contract&nbsp;
so the couple can establish boundaries.

00:10:51.423 --> 00:10:55.920
"If this relationship is gonna have any chance&nbsp;
at all, we have to lay down some ground rules."&nbsp;

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.222
"Great! I love rules."
"I do too!"

00:10:58.222 --> 00:11:02.040
It's a sweet gesture from Sheldon that&nbsp;
shows how much he truly loves his Meemaw.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:05.940
Of course, this sentimental moment doesn't&nbsp;
hold a candle to a certain other scene from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:10.380
"The Big Bang Theory" involving Sheldon and his&nbsp;
Meemaw. Fans are sure to find Amy's Christmas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:14.700
cookie gift extra bittersweet after watching&nbsp;
that particular episode of "Young Sheldon."

